NNAAMMEE
       dnssec‐cds - change DS records for a child zone based on CDS/CDNSKEY

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ddnnsssseecc--ccddss  [--aa alg...] [--cc class] [--DD] {--dd dsset-file} {--ff child-file}
       [--ii****[[eexxtteennssiioonn]]]] [[-s** start-time] [--TT ttl] [--uu] [--vv level] [--VV]  {do‐
       main}

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  ddnnsssseecc--ccddss  command changes DS records at a delegation point based
       on CDS or CDNSKEY records published in the child zone. If both CDS  and
       CDNSKEY  records  are  present in the child zone, the CDS is preferred.
       This enables a child zone to inform its parent of upcoming  changes  to
       its  key-signing  keys (KSKs); by polling periodically with ddnnsssseecc--ccddss,
       the parent can keep the DS  records  up-to-date  and  enable  automatic
       rolling of KSKs.

       Two input files are required. The _-_f _c_h_i_l_d_-_f_i_l_e option specifies a file
       containing  the  child's  CDS  and/or  CDNSKEY  records, plus RRSIG and
       DNSKEY records so that they can be authenticated. The  _-_d  _p_a_t_h  option
       specifies the location of a file containing the current DS records. For
       example,  this  could be a ddsssseett-- file generated by _d_n_s_s_e_c_-_s_i_g_n_z_o_n_e, or
       the output of _d_n_s_s_e_c_-_d_s_f_r_o_m_k_e_y, or the output  of  a  previous  run  of
       ddnnsssseecc--ccddss.

       The  ddnnsssseecc--ccddss  command uses special DNSSEC validation logic specified
       by _R_F_C _7_3_4_4. It requires that the CDS and/or CDNSKEY records be validly
       signed by a key represented in the existing DS records. This  is  typi‐
       cally the pre-existing KSK.

       For  protection  against  replay  attacks,  the signatures on the child
       records must not  be  older  than  they  were  on  a  previous  run  of
       ddnnsssseecc--ccddss. Their age is obtained from the modification time of the ddss‐‐
       sseett-- file, or from the _-_s option.

       To protect against breaking the delegation, ddnnsssseecc--ccddss ensures that the
       DNSKEY  RRset  can  be  verified  by  every key algorithm in the new DS
       RRset, and that the same set of keys are covered  by  every  DS  digest
       type.

       By  default, replacement DS records are written to the standard output;
       with the _-_i option the input file is overwritten in place. The replace‐
       ment DS records are the same as the existing records, when no change is
       required. The output can be empty if the  CDS/CDNSKEY  records  specify
       that the child zone wants to be insecure.

       WWAARRNNIINNGG::
          Be careful not to delete the DS records when ddnnsssseecc--ccddss fails!

       Alternatively,  :option`dnssec-cds -u` writes an _n_s_u_p_d_a_t_e script to the
       standard output. The _-_u and _-_i options can be used together to maintain
       a ddsssseett-- file as well as emit an _n_s_u_p_d_a_t_e script.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --aa aallggoorriitthhmm
              When converting CDS records to DS records, this option specifies
              the acceptable digest algorithms. This option can  be  repeated,
              so  that  multiple  digest types are allowed. If none of the CDS
              records use an acceptable digest type, ddnnsssseecc--ccddss  will  try  to
              use CDNSKEY records instead; if there are no CDNSKEY records, it
              reports an error.

              When converting CDNSKEY records to DS records, this option spec‐
              ifies  the  digest algorithm to use. It can be repeated, so that
              multiple DS records are created for each CDNSKEY records.

              The algorithm must be one of SHA-1, SHA-256, or  SHA-384.  These
              values  are  case-insensitive, and the hyphen may be omitted. If
              no algorithm is specified, the default is SHA-256 only.

       --cc ccllaassss
              This option specifies the DNS class of the zones.

       --DD     This option generates DS records from CDNSKEY  records  if  both
              CDS  and  CDNSKEY  records are present in the child zone. By de‐
              fault CDS records are preferred.

       --dd ppaatthh
              This specifies the location of the parent DS records.  The  path
              can  be the name of a file containing the DS records; if it is a
              directory, ddnnsssseecc--ccddss looks for a ddsssseett-- file for the domain in‐
              side the directory.

              To protect against replay attacks, child records are rejected if
              they were signed earlier than the modification time of  the  ddss‐‐
              sseett-- file. This can be adjusted with the _-_s option.

       --ff cchhiilldd--ffiillee
              This option specifies the file containing the child's CDS and/or
              CDNSKEY  records, plus its DNSKEY records and the covering RRSIG
              records, so that they can be authenticated.

              The examples below describe how to generate this file.

       --ii eexxtteennssiioonn
              This option updates the ddsssseett-- file in place, instead of writing
              DS records to the standard output.

              There must be no space between the _-_i and the extension.  If  no
              extension is provided, the old ddsssseett-- is discarded. If an exten‐
              sion  is  present,  a backup of the old ddsssseett-- file is kept with
              the extension appended to its filename.

              To protect against replay attacks, the modification time of  the
              ddsssseett--  file is set to match the signature inception time of the
              child records, provided that it is later than the file's current
              modification time.

       --ss ssttaarrtt--ttiimmee
              This option specifies  the  date  and  time  after  which  RRSIG
              records  become  acceptable. This can be either an absolute or a
              relative time. An absolute start time is indicated by  a  number
              in  YYYYMMDDHHMMSS notation; 20170827133700 denotes 13:37:00 UTC
              on August 27th, 2017. A time relative to the ddsssseett-- file is  in‐
              dicated with --NN, which is N seconds before the file modification
              time.  A  time  relative  to  the current time is indicated with
              nnooww++NN.

              If no start-time is specified, the modification time of the  ddss‐‐
              sseett-- file is used.

       --TT ttttll This  option  specifies  a TTL to be used for new DS records. If
              not specified, the default is the TTL of the old DS records.  If
              they  had  no  explicit TTL, the new DS records also have no ex‐
              plicit TTL.

       --uu     This option writes an _n_s_u_p_d_a_t_e script to  the  standard  output,
              instead of printing the new DS reords. The output is empty if no
              change is needed.

              Note:  The  TTL  of new records needs to be specified: it can be
              done in the original ddsssseett-- file, with the _-_T option,  or  using
              the _n_s_u_p_d_a_t_e ttttll command.

       --VV     This option prints version information.

       --vv lleevveell
              This  option sets the debugging level. Level 1 is intended to be
              usefully verbose for general users; higher levels  are  intended
              for developers.

       ddoommaaiinn This indicates the name of the delegation point/child zone apex.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
       The  ddnnsssseecc--ccddss command exits 0 on success, or non-zero if an error oc‐
       curred.

       If successful, the DS records may or may not need to be changed.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       Before  running  _d_n_s_s_e_c_-_s_i_g_n_z_o_n_e,  ensure  that  the  delegations   are
       up-to-date by running ddnnsssseecc--ccddss on every ddsssseett-- file.

       To fetch the child records required by ddnnsssseecc--ccddss, invoke _d_i_g as in the
       script  below. It is acceptable if the _d_i_g fails, since ddnnsssseecc--ccddss per‐
       forms all the necessary checking.

          for f in dsset-*
          do
              d=${f#dsset-}
              dig +dnssec +noall +answer $d DNSKEY $d CDNSKEY $d CDS |
              dnssec-cds -i -f /dev/stdin -d $f $d
          done

       When the parent zone is automatically signed by _n_a_m_e_d,  ddnnsssseecc--ccddss  can
       be  used with _n_s_u_p_d_a_t_e to maintain a delegation as follows.  The ddsssseett--
       file allows the script to avoid having to fetch and validate the parent
       DS records, and it maintains the replay attack protection time.

          dig +dnssec +noall +answer $d DNSKEY $d CDNSKEY $d CDS |
          dnssec-cds -u -i -f /dev/stdin -d $f $d |
          nsupdate -l

